    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e$2.48 a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e for a slow braised Hungarian classic with the sour cream finish already in it. Nobody in Omaha is selling you this plate for that.",
    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003ePaprika is the recipe, so buy it right.\u003c/strong\u003e Hungarian sweet paprika costs more up front but one container flavors many batches. Old, faded paprika tastes like red dust and will sink this dish.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eA quarter pound of bacon does a lot of work.\u003c/strong\u003e You are rendering it for fat and smoke, not eating bacon strips, so the smallest package you can find covers several batches.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eLight sour cream is cheaper and behaves better.\u003c/strong\u003e One sixteen ounce tub carries the batch, and tempering it in off the heat keeps it from breaking.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCanned sauerkraut beats the jar on price.\u003c/strong\u003e Two cans cover this and the long braise smooths out any difference."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Cut the pork shoulder into large cubes and pat them dry. Dice the onions and the green bell peppers, and drain the sauerkraut.",
                    "Render the chopped bacon in a heavy pot until the fat runs, then brown the pork cubes in that fat in batches and set them aside.",
                    "Cook the onions and peppers in the pot until soft, then add the garlic for a minute.",
                    "Pull the pot off the heat before you stir in the paprika. Paprika scorches fast over direct heat and turns bitter, and that single step is what separates a good goulash from a ruined one.",
                    "Return the pot to the burner, add the diced tomatoes, beef broth, caraway seeds and bay leaves, and bring it to a simmer.",
                    "Return the pork, cover, and braise low until the meat is fork tender, then stir in the sauerkraut and cook another stretch so it softens into the sauce.",
                    "Kill the heat, temper the sour cream with a ladle of the hot liquid, then stir it back into the pot. Cook the rice separately to serve underneath."
                ],
